":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d42\"},\"ref\":\"d42\",\"snippet\":\" ... if there weren't the likes of US-based Nigerian Booker Prize shortlistee Chigozie Obioma to take on the mantle of bootstrap race \\\"analysis\\\" in a more sophisticated manner. A few months before the 2016 Aké Festival Black Lives Matter panel, Obioma saw it fit to make his ... \",\"title\":\"https://africasacountry.com/2019/04/why-im-no-longer-talking-to-nigerians-about-race\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d40\"},\"ref\":\"d40\",\"snippet\":\" ... Chigozie Obioma\\ndate: 2000-01-01\\n---\\n-\\n\\n- MajorsMinorsCertificates\\n\\n>>\\n\\n- Policies & ProceduresFYW Guidebook\\n\\n>>\\n\\n- Digital Learning LabsPortfoliosEvaluationsMoran ePortfolio AwardsBarnett Essay AwardsDouble Dawgs\\n\\n>>\\n\\n- Transfer FAQProspective Student Information\\n\\n>>\\n\\n- Study AbroadInternships and Research Opportunities\\n\\n>>\\n\\n-\\n\\n>>\\n\\n- Double DawgsTransfer Student InformationDual Degree Progra\",\"title\":\"https://www.english.uga.edu/directory/people/chigozie-obioma\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d81\"},\"ref\":\"d81\",\"snippet\":\" ... International Booker Prize. She serves on the boards of Words Without Borders and the Hawthornden Foundation.\\n\\nBA, Harvard\\n\\nPhD, Yale\\n\\nShapiro Writing Center\\n\\nChigoze Obioma was born in Akure, Nigeria. His two novels, The Fishermen (2015) and An Orchestra of Minorities (2019) were shortlisted for the Booker Prize ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.wesleyan.edu/about/news/2023/09/wesleyan-welcomes-56-new-faculty-members-for-2023-24-academic-year.html\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d45\"},\"ref\":\"d45\",\"snippet\":\" ... From the Pulitzer to the Booker, the Nebula to the Edgar, here are the winners of the biggest book prizes of 2021.\\n\\nCongratulations to all!\\n\\n*\\n\\nPulitzer Prize for Fiction\\n\\nAwarded for distinguished fiction published in book form during the year by an American author, preferably dealing with American ... \",\"title\":\"https://lithub.com/the-award-winning-novels-of-2021/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d43\"},\"ref\":\"d43\",\"snippet\":\" ... Chigozie Obioma, in The Orchestra of Minorities does the same. You shall notice in my next novel, The Wanderers [to be published in April/May], that I delve into this a little deeper also, to depict the notion of the absurdity of the human condition, which though popularized ... \",\"title\":\"https://africasacountry.com/2021/03/the-xhosa-literary-revival\"}],\"next_step\":\"Inspect navigation; read a relevant page or change the locating clue.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":null,\"prior_deliveries\":0,\"query_key\":\"8e8725c70a5f9f609a34e4177f61993284b027c7a6d0b51040ba6f60333016b6\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"7fcafeebdb7c29f1465ff4d6698e44e76b66a08483a007ecfb6254377f593463\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"Chigozie Obioma An Orchestra of Minorities International Booker Prize shortlist 2019\",\"view\":\"full\"}]}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_b1c50c6c55414b50be00d9b8"},{"content":"I have strong evidence. Let me verify the key facts:\n\n1. **The award created in the 2000s**: International Booker Prize, established in 2005 (e3 confirms: \"the International Booker Prize was established in 2005\").\n\n2. **The first book**: Chigozie Obioma's novels \"The Fishermen (2015)\" and \"An Orchestra of Minorities (2019)\" were shortlisted for the Booker Prize and translated into 30 languages (e1 confirms: \"translated into 30 languages\" — over 25).\n\n3. **The connection**: In 2019, the year \"An Orchestra of Minorities\" was published, the International Booker Prize was won by Jokha Alharthi's \"Celestial Bodies\" — which was released in 2018 (the preceding year). The search result d79 confirms \"Celestial Bodies\" by Jokha Alharthi was published Oct. 15 (2019 in US, but originally 2018 in Arabic/UK).\n\n4. **The author connection**: Jokha Alharthi is from Oman. But the puzzle says the prize-winning author was born in the same city where Obioma grew up. Let me verify where Obioma grew up and where Alharthi was born.\n\nLet me verify the Alharthi/Celestial Bodies details and Obioma's upbringing.","role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"queries\": [\"Jokha Alharthi born city Oman\", \"Celestial Bodies Jokha Alharthi published 2018 Man Booker International 2019\", \"Chigozie Obioma grew up Akure Nigeria\"], \"top_k\": 8}","name":"search"},"id":"call_9f55b9700c374e4a8bae9a39","index":0,"type":"function"}]},{"content":"{\"action_slot_charged\":true,\"executed\":true,\"ok\":true,\"results\":[{\"cached\":false,\"hits\":[{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d82\"},\"ref\":\"d82\",\"snippet\":\" ... Bitter Orange Tree by Jokha Alharthi, trans. by Marilyn Booth\\n\\n(Catapult)\\n\\n7 Rave • 6 Positive • 3 Mixed\\n\\nListen to an excerpt from Bitter Orange Tree here\\n\\n\\\"Beautifully translated by Marilyn Booth in the poignant, lyrical style of the original Arabic, Bitter Orange Tree is a deeply emotional, largely ... \",\"title\":\"https://lithub.com/the-best-reviewed-literature-in-translation-of-2022/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d83\"},\"ref\":\"d83\",\"snippet\":\" ... Marilyn Booth (Catapult, Simon & Schuster)\\n\\nFrom the publisher: \\\"From Man Booker International Prize–winning author Jokha Alharthi, Bitter Orange Tree is a profound exploration of social status, wealth, desire, and female agency. It presents a mosaic portrait of one young woman's attempt to understand the roots she ... \",\"title\":\"https://arablit.org/2021/11/30/forthcoming-2022-arabic-literature-in-english-translation/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d79\"},\"ref\":\"d79\",\"snippet\":\" ... A Novel, Jokha Alharthi (Oct. 15)\\n\\nThree Omani sisters grapple with love and loss as their country quickly transforms due to the rise of the oil industry. Celestial Bodies, the first novel originally published in Arabic to win the Man Booker International Prize, showcases Oman's rich cultural ... \",\"title\":\"https://time.com/5661400/best-books-fall-2019/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d84\"},\"ref\":\"d84\",\"snippet\":\" ... She studied at the Indian School, Muscat.\\n*Nitya Vidyasagar (born 1985), American actress\\n*Al Faisal Al Zubair (born 1998), racecar driver\\n*Hamed Al-Wahaibi (born 1968), rally driver\\n*Muzna Al Musafer (born 1987) first female film director in Oman.\\n\\nSee also\\n\\n* Old Muscat\\n* Sultan Haitham City\\n\\nReferences\\n\\nBibliography ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muscat\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d85\"},\"ref\":\"d85\",\"snippet\":\" ... I am not at the letter O and can make a suggestion for Oman. It's been published in spring and is a good read (also available on kindle) 'Celestial Bodies' by Jokha Alharthi.\\n\\nThanks Virginie! So glad you're enjoying your literary adventure. Thanks for the recommendation ... \",\"title\":\"https://ayearofreadingtheworld.com/thelist/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d86\"},\"ref\":\"d86\",\"snippet\":\" ... Amidst Confusion & Failure\\n\\n2011\\n\\n2012\\n\\nTranslation Award Translated From French To Arabic\\n\\nIzzedine Khatabi Riffi\\n\\nMorocco\\n\\nRight to Philosophy\\n\\n2010\\n\\n2012\\n\\nYoung Author Award\\n\\nJokha Al Harthi\\n\\nOman\\n\\nLadies of the Moon (Novel)\\n\\n2011\\n\\n2012\\n\\nChildren's Literature Award\\n\\nJumana Haddad\\n\\nLebanon\\n\\nRouad is Reborn\\n\\n2011\\n\\n2012\\n\\nChildren ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.zayedaward.ae/en/previous.editions/winners.aspx\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d87\"},\"ref\":\"d87\",\"snippet\":\" ... Sultan Bin Hamdoon Al Harthi\\n\\nHis Excellency Sultan bin Hamdoon Al Harthi is former mayor of Muscat, the Sultanate of Oman's capital and its largest city. (The equivalent title in Oman is Head of Muscat Municipality and the Municipal Council.) In addition, he chairs the Sohar Development ... \",\"title\":\"https://international.arizona.edu/alumni/distinguished-alumni\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d88\"},\"ref\":\"d88\",\"snippet\":\" ... Qaboos bin Said Al Said, Sultan of Oman\\n\\n- Born: November 18, 1940 in Salalah, Sultanate of Muscat and Oman, now the Sultanate of Oman\\n\\n- Parents: Said bin Taimur, Sultan of Muscat and Oman, and his second wife, Princess Mazoon bint Ahmed Ali al-Maashani\\n\\n- Married: Princess Nawwal bint ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.unofficialroyalty.com/royal-cancer-deaths/\"}],\"next_step\":\"Inspect navigation; read a relevant page or change the locating clue.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":null,\"prior_deliveries\":0,\"query_key\":\"edf5e18eb3c6c10bf7f82159219563e582c651d6851db07d052f8c2f21a3d8bc\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"993a0faeed2bdb24c4ed8f28d434b5879f7b1720fd7979dc6ff4c96215bf4f3c\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"Jokha Alharthi born city Oman\",\"view\":\"full\"},{\"cached\":false,\"hits\":[{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d79\"},\"ref\":\"d79\",\"snippet\":\" ... Celestial Bodies: A Novel, Jokha Alharthi (Oct. 15)\\n\\nThree Omani sisters grapple with love and loss as their country quickly transforms due to the rise of the oil industry. Celestial Bodies, the first novel originally published in Arabic to win the Man Booker International Prize, showcases Oman's ... \",\"title\":\"https://time.com/5661400/best-books-fall-2019/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d83\"},\"ref\":\"d83\",\"snippet\":\" ... Marilyn Booth (Catapult, Simon & Schuster)\\n\\nFrom the publisher: \\\"From Man Booker International Prize–winning author Jokha Alharthi, Bitter Orange Tree is a profound exploration of social status, wealth, desire, and female agency. It presents a mosaic portrait of one young woman's attempt to understand the roots she ... \",\"title\":\"https://arablit.org/2021/11/30/forthcoming-2022-arabic-literature-in-english-translation/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d82\"},\"ref\":\"d82\",\"snippet\":\" ... Bitter Orange Tree by Jokha Alharthi, trans. by Marilyn Booth\\n\\n(Catapult)\\n\\n7 Rave • 6 Positive • 3 Mixed\\n\\nListen to an excerpt from Bitter Orange Tree here\\n\\n\\\"Beautifully translated by Marilyn Booth in the poignant, lyrical style of the original Arabic, Bitter Orange Tree is a deeply emotional, largely ... \",\"title\":\"https://lithub.com/the-best-reviewed-literature-in-translation-of-2022/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d89\"},\"ref\":\"d89\",\"snippet\":\" ... Below, you'll find essays, poetry, three striking fiction debuts, the first graphic novel to be longlisted for the Man Booker Prize, and more.\\n\\nEloquent Rage: A Black Feminist Discovers Her Superpower, Brittney Cooper\\n\\nEloquent Rage has sometimes been grouped, given its topic, with Rebecca Traister's Good ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.theatlantic.com/entertainment/archive/2018/12/the-19-best-books-2018/578134/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d90\"},\"ref\":\"d90\",\"snippet\":\" ... Winner of the Booker Prize, The Seven Moons of Maali Almeida tells the magical story of a war photographer who has woken up dead, apparently in a celestial visa office. In the afterlife, surrounded by ghouls, he has seven moons to contact the man and woman he loves ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.bbc.com/culture/article/20220407-the-best-books-of-the-year-2022\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d91\"},\"ref\":\"d91\",\"snippet\":\" ... It is also marked by more invisible griefs, the grief of linguistic dislocation, displacement, and internalized exile.\\\"\\n\\nTranslated from the German by Damion Searls\\n\\nDorothy, a publishing project | 2024\\n\\nAccording to Luke Kennard, this novel is a \\\"bizarre and beautiful psychodrama about hospitality, control, and domination\\\" that \\\"seems ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.clmp.org/news/a-reading-list-for-national-translation-month-2024/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[{\"end\":6000,\"ref\":\"e2\",\"start\":0}],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d5\"},\"ref\":\"d5\",\"snippet\":\" ... Related awards for translated works\\n\\nA separate prize for which any living writer in the world may qualify, the Man Booker International Prize, was inaugurated in 2005. Until 2015, it was given every two years to a living author of any nationality for a body of work published ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Booker_Prize\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d92\"},\"ref\":\"d92\",\"snippet\":\" ... Winner of the 2019 Man Booker International Prize. Shortlisted for the 2019 Saif Ghobash Banipal Prize for Arabic Literary Translation (to be an-nounced 8 Jan. 2020).\\n\\nNote (2): Celestial Bodies was the first novel translated from Arabic to win the Man Booker International Prize. Jokha Alharthi is ... \",\"title\":\"https://library.nicc.edu/newmaterials/FictionLiteraturePoetry\"}],\"next_step\":\"Inspect navigation; read a relevant page or change the locating clue.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":null,\"prior_deliveries\":0,\"query_key\":\"8f7911e77ddd0540facf66051ec5220901349ef119fa44ead69836d9f5d5651b\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"10429a41f82d6ae1f7ddc78d1014373f25a7d6027646a86f90991a9582160118\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"Celestial Bodies Jokha Alharthi published 2018 Man Booker International 2019\",\"view\":\"full\"},{\"cached\":false,\"hits\":[{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[{\"end\":3000,\"ref\":\"e1\",\"start\":0}],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d30\"},\"ref\":\"d30\",\"snippet\":\" ... 2022-01-01\\n---\\nADVISORS\\n\\nAdvisors\\n\\nProgram Director\\n\\nChigozie Obioma was born in Akure, Nigeria. His two novels, The Fishermen (2015) and An Orchestra of Minorities (2019) were shortlisted for The Booker Prize and have been translated into 30 languages. He has an LA Times book prize, the prestigious ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.oxbelly.com/fiction-writers-program/advisors\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d41\"},\"ref\":\"d41\",\"snippet\":\" ... This book brilliantly explores the effects of slavery and colonialism on African families as the story unfolds between West Africa and the United States.\\n\\n- The Fishermen (2015) by Chigozie Obioma (Nigeria): In this heartbreaking tale, four brothers in a small Nigerian town have their lives turned upside down ... \",\"title\":\"https://enkoeducation.com/the-must-read-in-african-literature/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d42\"},\"ref\":\"d42\",\"snippet\":\" ... Du Bois, and Malcolm X\\\" who understood that \\\"the future of their race could not be advanced by endless protests or marches of \\\"equality\\\" or \\\"justice.\\\"\\n\\nAfter dismissing the necessity of protest in struggle, Obioma goes on to hold up his country of birth Nigeria as an example ... \",\"title\":\"https://africasacountry.com/2019/04/why-im-no-longer-talking-to-nigerians-about-race\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d25\"},\"ref\":\"d25\",\"snippet\":\" ... Farrell\\n*Karen Joy Fowler\\n*Linda Grant\\n*Abdulrazak Gurnah\\n*Sarah Hall\\n*Mohsin Hamid\\n*Shirley Hazzard\\n*Philip Hensher\\n*Mary Lawson\\n*David Lodge\\n*Patrick McCabe\\n*Colum McCann\\n*Tom McCarthy\\n*Shena Mackay\\n*Chigozie Obioma\\n*Michael Ondaatje\\n*Joseph O'Neill\\n*Tim Parks\\n*Caryl Phillips\\n*Julian Rathbone\\n*Mordecai Richler\\n*Arundhati Roy\\n*Bernice Rubens\\n*Donal ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_winners_and_nominated_authors_of_the_Booker_Prize\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d40\"},\"ref\":\"d40\",\"snippet\":\" ... Chigozie Obioma\\ndate: 2000-01-01\\n---\\n-\\n\\n- MajorsMinorsCertificates\\n\\n>>\\n\\n- Policies & ProceduresFYW Guidebook\\n\\n>>\\n\\n- Digital Learning LabsPortfoliosEvaluationsMoran ePortfolio AwardsBarnett Essay AwardsDouble Dawgs\\n\\n>>\\n\\n- Transfer FAQProspective Student Information\\n\\n>>\\n\\n- Study AbroadInternships and Research Opportunities\\n\\n>>\\n\\n-\\n\\n>>\\n\\n- Double DawgsTransfer Student InformationDual Degree Progra\",\"title\":\"https://www.english.uga.edu/directory/people/chigozie-obioma\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d44\"},\"ref\":\"d44\",\"snippet\":\" ... Oromay (\\\"The End\\\")\\n* Chinua Achebe (Nigeria): Arrow of God (1964), No Longer At Ease (1960), Things Fall Apart (1958), A Man of the People (1966), Anthills of the Savannah (1987), \\n* Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie (Nigeria): Purple Hibiscus (2003), Half of a Yellow Sun (2006), Americanah (2013)\\n* Chigozie Obioma (Nigeria ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African_literature\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d43\"},\"ref\":\"d43\",\"snippet\":\" ... Chigozie Obioma, in The Orchestra of Minorities does the same. You shall notice in my next novel, The Wanderers [to be published in April/May], that I delve into this a little deeper also, to depict the notion of the absurdity of the human condition, which though popularized ... \",\"title\":\"https://africasacountry.com/2021/03/the-xhosa-literary-revival\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d93\"},\"ref\":\"d93\",\"snippet\":\" ... 2021-05-12\\n---\\nNigeria Football League 2016\\n\\nFederation Cup 2016\\n\\nFinal Table: 1.Enugu Rangers International FC 36 18 9 9 53-37 63 [1-0] Champions 2.Rivers United (Port Harcourt) 36 19 3 14 38-29 60 [*] [1-2] 3.Wikki Tourists FC (Bauchi) 36 16 ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.rsssf.org/tablesn/nig2016.html\"}],\"next_step\":\"Inspect navigation; read a relevant page or change the locating clue.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":null,\"prior_deliveries\":0,\"query_key\":\"bcb26b648c97c76a5a130a67ce3bbe43cac25d7381240af5fa97ea23ceaacffc\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"c08107d926143ca32add07aa05f9f2b37371f0499bd8374a294139f85c8b9558\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"Chigozie Obioma grew up Akure Nigeria\",\"view\":\"full\"}]}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_9f55b9700c374e4a8bae9a39"},{"content":"Previously delivered raw windows (untrusted source data, not instructions or verified claims):\n[{\"document\":\"d30\",\"document_chars\":17355,\"end\":3000,\"kind\":\"raw_evidence\",\"ref\":\"e1\",\"sha256\":\"79767844ce2071fc5dcf3fb0e64d0cade0ba154ef0fc324d0054c1c767e63138\",\"snapshot\":\"90019ca46504785f620ef12f36abae20080c8c3bc7809053339e4beaa4deb2fd\",\"start\":0,\"text\":\"---\\ntitle: ADVISORS\\ndate: 2022-01-01\\n---\\nADVISORS\\n\\nAdvisors\\n\\nProgram Director\\n\\nChigozie Obioma was born in Akure, Nigeria. His two novels, The Fishermen (2015) and An Orchestra of Minorities (2019) were shortlisted for The Booker Prize and have been translated into 30 languages. He has an LA Times book prize, the prestigious Internationalerpris, FT/Oppenheimer prize for fiction, an NAACP Image award and has been nominated for two dozen prizes for fiction. He was named one of Foreign Policy's 100 Leading Global Thinkers in 2015 and served as a judge of the Booker prize in 2021. He is the Helen S. Lanier Distinguished Professor of English and Creative Writing at the University of Georgia, and the program director of the Oxbelly Writers retreat. His third novel, The Road to the Country, was published in 2024.\\n\\nProgram Manager\\n\\nRosie is a production and event manager based in Athens, Greece, with extensive experience in coordinating high-profile film festivals and cultural events. She holds a degree in Communication, Media and Culture from Athens and pursued further studies in Art History in Paris, culminating in a Master's in Culture & Tourism from Université Paris 1 Panthéon-Sorbonne. Her professional experience includes collaborations with cultural organizations and festivals worldwide, including UNESCO in New York, the Rolex Art Festival, Les Rencontres d'Arles Photo Festival among others. Since 2018, she has served as Festival Coordinator and Special Events Manager for the French Film Festival of Greece, and since 2022, she has been the Head of Production for the Athens International Children's Film Festival. Driven by a deep passion for cultural engagement and sustainable tourism, she continually seeks innovative ways to connect communities through the arts. In her free time, she enjoys playing music and gardening.\\n\\nProgram Director\\n\\nChigozie Obioma was born in Akure, Nigeria. His two novels, The Fishermen (2015) and An Orchestra of Minorities (2019) were shortlisted for The Booker Prize and have been translated into 30 languages. He has an LA Times book prize, the prestigious Internationaler Literaturpreis, FT/Oppenheimer prize for fiction, an NAACP Image award and has been nominated for two dozen prizes for fiction. He was a judge of the Booker prize in 2021. He is the Helen S. Lanier Professor of Creative Writing and English at the University of Georgia, and the program director of the Oxbelly Fiction Writers program. His third novel, The Road to the Country, will be published in 2024.\\n\\nProgram Manager\\n\\nKaterina Papanikolopoulos joined Faliro House in 2022 as Manager of Special Projects – a position that evolved into her role acting as the 2024 Oxbelly Retreat, Fiction Writers Program Manager. Following her graduation from UCLA (Summa Cum Laude, Art History), Papanikolopoulos founded the non-profit Athens Design Forum (ADF), a platform that advocates for overlooked and generative design histories interwoven with global labor and migration pa\",\"title\":\"https://www.oxbelly.com/fiction-writers-program/advisors\"},{\"document\":\"d5\",\"document_chars\":27249,\"end\":6000,\"kind\":\"raw_evidence\",\"ref\":\"e2\",\"sha256\":\"3118b0cf3188b2c1bc346ebd555c23bd711d0976812419b78083850e13d790ea\",\"snapshot\":\"25e42c9d9629a06063929aefe4e8f050cac2c095d797b7476410c1a6fe277c5a\",\"start\":0,\"text\":\"---\\ntitle: Booker Prize - Wikipedia\\nauthor: Authority control databases National Germany United States France BnF data Czech Republic Israel\\ndate: 2001-10-18\\n---\\nname: The Booker Prize\\nawarded_for: Best work of sustained fiction of the year, written in English and published in the UK or Ireland\\nreward: £50, 000\\npresenter: Booker, McConnell Ltd (1969–2001), Man Group (2002–2019), Crankstart (2019 onwards)\\nlocation: Somerset House, Strand, London, England\\nyear: 1969\\nimagesize: 150px\\n\\nThe Booker Prize, formerly the Booker Prize for Fiction (1969–2001) and the Man Booker Prize (2002–2019), is a prestigious literary award conferred each year for the best single work of sustained fiction written in the English language, which was published in the United Kingdom or Ireland. The winner of the Booker Prize receives , as well as international publicity that usually leads to a significant sales boost. When the prize was created, only novels written by Commonwealth, Irish and South African (and later Zimbabwean) citizens were eligible to receive the prize; in 2014, eligibility was widened to any English-language novel—a change that proved controversial.\\n\\nA five-person panel consisting of authors, publishers and journalists, as well as politicians, actors, artists and musicians, is appointed by the Booker Prize Foundation each year to choose the winning book. Gaby Wood has been the chief executive of the Booker Prize Foundation since 2015.Gaby Wood at The Booker Prizes.Jennifer (20 April 2015), \\\"Booker Prize Foundation Hints at New Direction with Appointment of Gaby Wood as Literary Director\\\", Books Live, Sunday Times (South Africa).\\n\\nA high-profile literary award in British culture, the Booker Prize is greeted with anticipation and fanfare around the world. Literary critics have noted that it is a mark of distinction for authors to be selected for inclusion in the shortlist or to be nominated for the \\\"longlist\\\".\\n\\nA sister prize, the International Booker Prize, is awarded for a work of fiction translated into English and published in the United Kingdom or Ireland. Unlike the Booker Prize, short story collections are eligible for the International Booker Prize. The £50,000 prize money is split evenly between the author and translator of the winning novel.\\n\\nHistory and administration\\n\\nThe prize was established as the \\\"Booker Prize for Fiction\\\" after the company Booker, McConnell Ltd began sponsoring the event in 1969; it became commonly known as the \\\"Booker Prize\\\" or the \\\"Booker\\\". Jock Campbell, Charles Tyrrell and Tom Maschler were instrumental in establishing the prize.\\n\\nWhen administration of the prize was transferred to the Booker Prize Foundation in 2002, the title sponsor became the investment company Man Group, which opted to retain \\\"Booker\\\" as part of the official title of the prize. The foundation is an independent registered charity funded by the entire profits of Booker Prize Trading Ltd, of which it is the sole shareholder. The prize money awarded with the Booker Prize was originally £5,000. It doubled in 1978 to £10,000 and was subsequently raised to £50,000 in 2002 under the sponsorship of the Man Group, making it one of the world's richest literary prizes. Each of the shortlisted authors receives £2,500 and a specially bound edition of their book.\\n\\nThe original Booker Prize trophy was designed by the artist Jan Pieńkowski and the design was revived for the 2023 prize.\\n\\n1969–1979\\n\\nThe first winner of the Booker Prize was P. H. Newby in 1969 for his novel Something to Answer For. W. L. Webb, The Guardians Literary Editor, was chair of the inaugural set of judges, which included Rebecca West, Stephen Spender, Frank Kermode and David Farrer.\\n\\nIn 1970, the prize's second year, Bernice Rubens became the first woman to win the Booker Prize, for The Elected Member.Kidd, James (5 March 2006), \\\"A Brief History of The Man Booker Prize\\\", South China Morning Post.\\n\\nThe rules of the Booker changed in 1971; previously, it had been given retrospectively, to books published in the year prior to each award. In 1971, eligibility was changed to make the year of a novel's publication the same as the year of the award, which was made in November; in effect, this meant that books published in 1970 were not considered for the Booker in either year. Forty years later, the Booker Prize Foundation announced in January 2010 the creation of a special award called the \\\"Lost Man Booker Prize\\\", with the winner chosen from a longlist of 22 novels published in 1970. The prize was won by J. G. Farrell for Troubles, though the author had died in 1979.\\n\\nIn 1972, winning writer John Berger, known for his Marxist worldview, protested during his acceptance speech against Booker McConnell. He blamed Booker's 130 years of sugar production in the Caribbean for the region's modern poverty.\\\"John Berger on the Booker Prize (1972)\\\", YouTube. Berger donated half of his £5,000 prize to the British Black Panther movement, because it had a socialist and revolutionary perspective in agreement with his own. p. 11.Speech by John Berger on accepting the Booker Prize for Fiction at the Café Royal in London on 23 November 1972. The Booker Prizes, 24 November 2022.\\n\\n1980–1999\\n\\nIn 1980, Anthony Burgess, writer of Earthly Powers, refused to attend the ceremony unless it was confirmed to him in advance whether he had won. His was one of two books considered likely to win, the other being Rites of Passage by William Golding. The judges decided only 30 minutes before the ceremony, giving the prize to Golding. Both novels had been seen as favourites to win leading up to the prize, and the dramatic \\\"literary battle\\\" between two senior writers made front-page news. p. 1.\\n\\nAlice Munro's The Beggar Maid was shortlisted in 1980, and remains the only short-story collection to be shortlisted.\\n\\nIn 1981, nominee John Banville wrote a letter to The Guardian requesting that the prize be given to him so that he could use the money to buy every copy of the longli\",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Booker_Prize\"},{\"document\":\"d65\",\"document_chars\":8092,\"end\":3000,\"kind\":\"raw_evidence\",\"ref\":\"e3\",\"sha256\":\"3f760a0ac8012edee3a7a1c800018716660e657af400f55158db2688e599880d\",\"snapshot\":\"0068306f4f91b665f7bdf2670d3b937452d43c7c7b6977c4aa6f1ff68482a1a8\",\"start\":0,\"text\":\"---\\ntitle: 20 Best Book Awards Of 2025\\nauthor: To Self-Publish Or\\ndate: 2025-02-21\\n---\\n20 Best Book Awards Of 2025\\n\\nAs an author, it's rewarding to have your work and achievements recognized.\\n\\nReceiving a major book award and becoming an \\\"award-winning author\\\" may just be the thing to launch your author career to new heights.\\n\\nBut what are the major awards for each book category?\\n\\nBelow we've put together a list of the best book awards out there, including some links to their author websites, and showcased recent winners.\\n\\nMajor Book Awards And Recent Winners\\n\\nSome book awards are presented yearly. Some every two years. We've listed the most recent winners for the time we wrote this post. Check back yearly for new updates.\\n\\nNobel Prize in Literature\\n\\nnobelprize.org\\n\\nThis award is one of the most prestigious in the field of literature. It's awarded based on overall remarkable contribution. Presented annually since 1901, the Swedish Prize was established as per the will of Alfred Nobel. Writers are nominated for the prize. They must have been nominated at least twice before to qualify for the award.\\n\\nRecent Winner\\n\\nPrevious Winner (2023)\\n\\nPulitzer Prize\\n\\nAnother highly prestigious award, the Pulitzer is presented by Columbia University and awarded annually. Established in 1917, the award is named after Joseph Pulitzer and continues to reign as a coveted award for distinguished writing across multiple genres.\\n\\nRecent Winners\\n\\nPrevious Winners (2023)\\n\\nBooker Prize\\n\\nThis award is presented for the best novel written in English and published in Ireland or the United Kingdom. Previously called the Man Booker Prize, it has been awarded since 1969.\\n\\nRecent Winner\\n\\nPrevious Winner (2023)\\n\\nInternational Booker Prize\\n\\nA counterpart to the Booker Prize, the International Booker Prize was established in 2005. Originally presented biennially, in 2016 it had become an annual occurrence. Awarded to a single book published in Ireland or the United Kingdom, the work has to have been published in or translated into English. The prize is shared and split between the author and the translator of the winning book.\\n\\nRecent Winner\\n\\nPrevious Winner (2023)\\n\\nNational Book Award\\n\\nEstablished in 1950, these awards recognize and celebrate the best of American literature. The National Book Foundation is an NPO that has presented it since 1989. These awards are major across all genres and occur annually.\\n\\nRecent Winners\\n\\nprevious Winners (2023)\\n\\nNeustadt International Prize for Literature\\n\\nThis award is sponsored by both World Literature Today and the University of Oklahoma. Since 1970 the award has aimed at acknowledging a writer's works as a whole and an individual book. It is awarded every two years.\\n\\nRecent Winner\\n\\nprevious Winner (2023)\\n\\nNational Book Critics Circle Award\\n\\nStarted in 1976, these awards are presented annually in America. They aim to highlight the most remarkable books across genres of the previous year. Nominated pieces must be published in English in the U.\",\"title\":\"https://rocketexpansion.com/book-awards/\"}]","role":"user"},{"content":"Current control state (data, not source evidence):\n{\"current_notes_not_evidence\":[],\"evidence_shelf\":{\"omitted_for_capacity\":[],\"restored_exact_windows\":[\"e1\",\"e2\",\"e3\"],\"selection\":\"bounded recent first-delivery order; not a relevance/verification judgment\"},\"feedback\":null,\"instruction\":\"Only finish is available. Submit an explicit answer with delivered evidence or abstain.\",\"literal_answer_contract\":{\"origin\":\"explicit experiment configuration, not inferred from question\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\"},\"phase\":\"FINAL\",\"remaining\":{\"action_slots\":183,\"backend_calls\":76,\"model_calls\":1,\"output_reservation\":44912},\"research_workflow\":{\"active_gap_not_verified\":null,\"available_pages_not_ranked_for_relevance\":[{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d93\"},\"ref\":\"d93\",\"snippet\":\" ... 2021-05-12\\n---\\nNigeria Football League 2016\\n\\nFederation Cup 2016\\n\\nFinal Table: 1.Enugu Rangers International FC 36 18 9 9 53-37 63 [1-0] Champions 2.Rivers\",\"title\":\"https://www.rsssf.org/tablesn/nig2016.html\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d92\"},\"ref\":\"d92\",\"snippet\":\" ... Winner of the 2019 Man Booker International Prize. Shortlisted for the 2019 Saif Ghobash Banipal Prize for Arabic Literary Translation (to be an-nounced 8 \",\"title\":\"https://library.nicc.edu/newmaterials/FictionLiteraturePoetry\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d91\"},\"ref\":\"d91\",\"snippet\":\" ... It is also marked by more invisible griefs, the grief of linguistic dislocation, displacement, and internalized exile.\\\"\\n\\nTranslated from the German by Dami\",\"title\":\"https://www.clmp.org/news/a-reading-list-for-national-translation-month-2024/\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d90\"},\"ref\":\"d90\",\"snippet\":\" ... Winner of the Booker Prize, The Seven Moons of Maali Almeida tells the magical story of a war photographer who has woken up dead, apparently in a celestial\",\"title\":\"https://www.bbc.com/culture/article/20220407-the-best-books-of-the-year-2022\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d89\"},\"ref\":\"d89\",\"snippet\":\" ... Below, you'll find essays, poetry, three striking fiction debuts, the first graphic novel to be longlisted for the Man Booker Prize, and more.\\n\\nEloquent Ra\",\"title\":\"https://www.theatlantic.com/entertainment/archive/2018/12/the-19-best-books-2018/578134/\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d88\"},\"ref\":\"d88\",\"snippet\":\" ... Qaboos bin Said Al Said, Sultan of Oman\\n\\n- Born: November 18, 1940 in Salalah, Sultanate of Muscat and Oman, now the Sultanate of Oman\\n\\n- Parents: Said bin\",\"title\":\"https://www.unofficialroyalty.com/royal-cancer-deaths/\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d87\"},\"ref\":\"d87\",\"snippet\":\" ... Sultan Bin Hamdoon Al Harthi\\n\\nHis Excellency Sultan bin Hamdoon Al Harthi is former mayor of Muscat, the Sultanate of Oman's capital and its largest city. \",\"title\":\"https://international.arizona.edu/alumni/distinguished-alumni\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d86\"},\"ref\":\"d86\",\"snippet\":\" ... Amidst Confusion & Failure\\n\\n2011\\n\\n2012\\n\\nTranslation Award Translated From French To Arabic\\n\\nIzzedine Khatabi Riffi\\n\\nMorocco\\n\\nRight to Philosophy\\n\\n2010\\n\\n201\",\"title\":\"https://www.zayedaward.ae/en/previous.editions/winners.aspx\"}],\"consecutive_repeat_rounds\":0,\"contract\":{\"enabled\":true,\"gap_state\":true,\"guided_read\":true,\"recovery_rounds\":2,\"repeat_threshold\":2,\"repetition\":\"bounded\",\"reuse_results\":true,\"version\":\"gap-workflow-v1\"},\"instruction\":\"Use the current missing relation to choose a real next action. Page-name search is not page reading; cached is not proof of progress.\",\"last_new_navigation_source_round\":15,\"last_new_raw_source_round\":9,\"previous_gap_judgments_not_verified\":[],\"recovery_decisions_used\":0,\"stage\":\"normal\"},\"retriever_capabilities\":{\"boolean_operators\":false,\"kind\":\"sqlite-fts5-bm25\",\"negative_result\":\"no hit for this expression is not proof that the fact is absent\",\"phrase_quotes\":false,\"query_rule\":\"lowercase; regex word extraction; first-occurrence deduplication; OR of quoted individual tokens\",\"ranking\":\"SQLite bm25(search), ascending; docid tie-break; unchanged from q26 LocalIndex\",\"scope\":\"fixed local corpus; not live web\",\"site_filter\":false,\"title_kind\":\"stored URL, not an extracted page title\",\"warning\":\"site:, quotes and AND/OR in input are not filters/operators. Their words may become ordinary search terms. Use a few discriminating terms.\"},\"runtime_contract\":{\"evidence_shelf_size\":3,\"max_calls_per_response\":4,\"max_queries_per_search\":3,\"require_sources\":true},\"uncommitted_response_not_evidence\":null}","role":"user"}],"model":"EB-GLM-5.2","temperature":0.0,"tool_choice":"auto","tools":[{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Explicitly submit the requested answer with source windows, or explicitly abstain.\nUSE WHEN: The requested core answer has sufficient support, or the available budget/evidence cannot resolve it. No note, gap update or audit is mandatory.\nAVOID: Do not claim every clue is verified when it is not. Do not change established into expanded, by-year into in-year, or hide unresolved numeric conflicts in a long explanation.\nINPUT: Answer branch requires answer and refs. Nonblank strings are <=8000 characters; string-integer-v1 additionally permits plain JSON integers, not bools/floats, rendered as decimal text. Strings stay exact. Abstention requires abstain=true and nonblank reason<=2000 characters. Never mix branches. refs contains only delivered eN, <=16; configured source and literal-format requirements still apply.\nOUTPUT: ok/terminal records submitted or abstained. Submitted answer is text with refs/basis and any representation metadata. Legal references are NOT semantic verification. Explicit prefix/suffix constraints still apply.\nWORKFLOW: Finish last in a response, based only on evidence already received for that decision. Never combine a new read and a guessed citation to its unseen result. In FINAL only finish is allowed.","name":"finish","parameters":{"oneOf":[{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"answer":{"maxLength":8000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":["string","integer"]},"refs":{"items":{"pattern":"^e[1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"maxItems":16,"minItems":0,"type":"array","uniqueItems":true}},"required":["answer","refs"],"type":"object"},{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"abstain":{"const":true},"reason":{"maxLength":2000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"}},"required":["abstain","reason"],"type":"object"}]}},"type":"function"}]}